-- title:
--   The dyadic affine group $\langle y \mapsto 2y,\ y \mapsto y+1 \rangle$ contains no $\mathbb{Z}^2$
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ and $T$ be the order-preserving bijections of $\mathbb{R}$ given by $M(y) = 2y$ and $T(y) = y + 1$, and let $G = \langle M, T \rangle$ be the subgroup they generate inside the group of order-isomorphisms of $\mathbb{R}$. Concretely
--
--   $$G = \{\, y \mapsto 2^{n} y + b \;:\; n \in \mathbb{Z},\ b \in \mathbb{Z}[1/2] \,\},$$
--
--   the affine group of the dyadic rationals. Its two generators satisfy $M T M^{-1} = T^{2}$, the defining relation of the Baumslag-Solitar group $BS(1,2)$.
--
--   The assertion is that $G$ contains no free abelian subgroup of rank two: there is **no** pair $u, v \in G$ for which both
--
--   $$u v = v u \qquad \text{and} \qquad (m,n) \longmapsto u^{m} v^{n}, \qquad \mathbb{Z}^{2} \to G,$$
--
--   hold, the second being injectivity of that map. Commutativity is what makes the map a homomorphism, so a pair satisfying both conjuncts is exactly a copy of $\mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z}$ sitting inside $G$; the statement denies that any such pair exists. It produces no subgroup object and no isomorphism, and it says nothing about $u$ and $v$ beyond the two conjuncts.
--
--   **Role.** Brin-Squier's Theorem (3.2) concludes that a non-abelian subgroup of $\mathrm{PLF}'(\mathbb{R})$ — the piecewise-linear homeomorphisms of $\mathbb{R}$ with finitely many breakpoints that are *translations* near $-\infty$ and near $+\infty$ — contains a free abelian subgroup of infinite rank. The group $G$ above is non-abelian, and each of its elements is piecewise linear with no breakpoints at all, so $G \le \mathrm{PLF}(\mathbb{R})$; but its elements have slope $2^{n}$ rather than slope one at the two ends, placing $G$ outside that hypothesis. This statement records that the conclusion genuinely fails for $G$, and hence that the slope-one hypothesis cannot be weakened to plain piecewise linearity.
--
--   **Formalization note.** $M$ and $T$ are universally quantified and pinned by the pointwise hypotheses $M y = 2y$ and $T y = y + 1$, which determine them uniquely, so the statement needs no separate definitions. The ambient group is `ℝ ≃o ℝ`, the order-isomorphisms of $\mathbb{R}$ under composition, and $u^{m}$ for $m \in \mathbb{Z}$ is the integer power in that group.
-- source:
--   M. G. Brin and C. C. Squier, Groups of piecewise linear homeomorphisms of the real line, Invent. math. 79 (1985), 485-498, doi:10.1007/BF01388519. The two generators are Definition (2.1a) (M_p : t -> pt) and Definition (2.1b) (T_a : t -> t+a), p. 488, where the paper also notes that each M_p and T_a belongs to PLF(R); the relation used here is Lemma (2.2b), p. 489, T_a M_p = M_p T_{pa} (their notation composes left to right), which at p = 2, a = 1 is the defining relation of BS(1,2). PROVENANCE: the conclusion formalized here -- that the group generated by M_2 and T_1 contains no free abelian subgroup of rank two -- is NOT stated in Brin-Squier, and no reference for it in the literature has been located. It is recorded here as the witness that the slope-one hypothesis of their Theorem (3.2), p. 494, cannot be weakened to plain piecewise linearity.

import Mathlib

namespace BrinSquier

theorem bs12_no_zsq
    (M T : ℝ ≃o ℝ) (hM : ∀ y : ℝ, M y = 2 * y) (hT : ∀ y : ℝ, T y = y + 1) :
    ¬ ∃ u ∈ Subgroup.closure ({M, T} : Set (ℝ ≃o ℝ)),
        ∃ v ∈ Subgroup.closure ({M, T} : Set (ℝ ≃o ℝ)),
          u * v = v * u ∧
            Function.Injective (fun p : ℤ × ℤ => u ^ p.1 * v ^ p.2) := by
  sorry

end BrinSquier
